SUMMARY	CSV exports now contain every row of a report, however large, instead of stopping at 10,000.
WHY	Northwind's monthly usage export stopped at 10,000 rows, so totals did not match the dashboard. The export now reads the report page by page until the last page.
RISK	low	Only the CSV export calls the new cursor. Memory stays flat because pages are written as they arrive. The new test covers 25,000 rows.
ASK	-
STEP	A cursor over every page	src/reports/rows.ts:7-18	Reports are stored in pages. The old fetchRows asked for one page of 10,000 and stopped there; rowCursor follows each page's next link until there is none.
THINK	src/reports/rows.ts:14	const page = await query(reportId, { limit: opts.pageSize, after });	If the report changes while it is exported, a page can shift. Is the cursor stable for a running export?	Is the after cursor stable if rows are added during an export, or could a row show up twice?	snapshot the report first | document it | order by a stable id
STEP	Stream the export	src/reports/export.ts:16-38	The export writes each page as it arrives and waits for drain when the client reads slowly, so a 1 GB report never sits in memory.
THINK	src/reports/export.ts:8	const PAGE_SIZE = 2_000;	Smaller pages mean more queries for big reports. Is 2,000 measured or a guess?	Did you measure 2,000? A larger page could halve the queries for big exports.	keep 2,000 | measure 5,000 | make it a setting
THINK	src/reports/export.ts:27	if (!header) {	A report with no rows never writes a header, so the file is empty.	An empty report now downloads an empty file without the header row. Is that intended?	write the header up front | keep it empty
STEP	Tests for big reports	src/reports/export.test.ts:4-15	A 25,000 row report comes out with all rows plus one header, and the header is written once.
